      "textEn": "322\n\nUnwillingly — such a person who is unwilling to spend his wealth; also— certainly; Kadaryasya— extremely miserly being;\nOf a miser; Kutumbinah— surrounded by family members; Uranak-vat— like a lamb; Sarkarshyamanam— although safe;\nMishat— who is watching; Api— also, he; Haranti— they forcibly snatch away; Hey Rajan, in this material world, the family members who are called by names like wife, son, etc., in reality\nact like wolves and jackals. The shepherd wants to protect his sheep to the best of his ability,\nbut wolves and foxes forcibly take them away. Similarly, although\na miserly man wants to carefully guard his wealth, his family\nanimals forcefully take away all his properties even while he is aware. Meaning: A Hindi poet has said—The day is a dakini (female spirit), the night is a tigress, and the eyelids lick.\nThey suck. The wife is like a witch during the day and a tigress at night.\nHer only task\nis to suck the blood of her husband day and night. All day, the husband earns by working hard.\nThe money goes into household expenses. In the night, due to sexual pleasure, the husband sheds his blood in the form of semen. In this way, his blood is sucked by his wife day and night, but he remains so mad\nthat he takes very good care of her. Similarly, children are also like wolves, jackals,\nand foxes. Just as wolves, jackals, and foxes, even when the shepherd is alert,\ntake the lambs away, similarly, children also take their father's wealth, although the care of this wealth\nis taken by the father himself. In this way, even though family members are called wife and children, in reality, they are\nrobbers.\nJust as a seed that has been sown is burnt, but the field is again sown, so is the house like a cow, a grain of barley, and a cowherd.\nThe house and the field are the field of action, in which actions arise, and this is the abode of desire. 4>. Word As—in the manner; Hi—certainly; Anuvatsaram—every year; Krishyamanam—once the c