      "textEn": "331\n\nThat he should dedicate this extra wealth to the service of God. From this, the bound soul, this world, and\nShri Krishna will be pleased, and this is the purpose of this life. One-sided attachment, like a stream of water, is more painful than the simultaneous attachment of both. 13. Meaning of the word\n\nOnce—sometimes; from false discourse—those who are against the Vedic rules and give birth to many religious sects; Nikrut-matih—whose intellect has reached such a low level that they deny the existence of God;\nVyudak-stroh-tah—in rivers without abundant water; Sakhan-vat—like jumping; Ubhayathah—from both sides; Api—even though; Dukha-\nDam—causing sorrow; Pakhandam—the path of non-God, Pakhand; Abhiyati—follows; Anusaran—follows. Sometimes, in this worldly bondage, to get freedom from one's sufferings, the bound soul receives cheap blessings from hypocritical people. Then, through their contact, his mind gets corrupted. This is like jumping into a shallow\nriver. The result is that his head bursts.\nIn this way,\nhe is not able to calm the sorrows he receives from the heat, and he suffers loss from both sides.\nIs. This deluded, bound soul goes to the shelter of so-called saints and masters who\ngive teachings against the Vedas. But from these, he neither gets benefit in the present nor in the future.\nHe gets it. Meaning: Skeletons keep making their way to the self-realisation. For some material gain,\nto obtain cheap blessings from these deceitful ascetics and yogis, a bound soul goes to their refuge,\nbut he neither receives spiritual nor material benefit. In this era, there are many cunning\npeople who cheat by showing magic tricks. They even make gold to amaze their followers,\nand their followers consider them gods. In Kali Yuga, this type of fraud is prevalent. Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur has described the true Guru in this way—\nThe world is like a forest fire, a mountain of suffering,\nA dense cloud of compassion and fear.\nI bow to the lotus feet of the Guru, the ocean of virtues. Man should seek refuge in a guru who can quell the burning fire of this material world"
