      "textEn": "334\n\nSometimes—occasionally; Kal-vish-mit—made crooked by time; Raj-kul—ruling class; Rakshasa—demons; By; Apahrut—taken away; Priya-tam—most beloved; Dhan—as wealth; Asu-h—whose life-air;\nPramrutakah—dead; Iva—like, similar; Vigat-jeev-lakshanah—devoid of all the characteristics of life; Asthe—remains. The rulers are always like vampire-demons. These government officials sometimes become angry with the living beings,\nand they take away all their accumulated wealth. In this way, throughout one's life,\nby losing the accumulated capital, a bound soul becomes discouraged. Actually, this is like a life for him.\nIt is like a death. Meaning: The word rajakula-rakshasa is very precise. The composition of Shrimad Bhagwat happened five thousand years ago.\nIt happened. Even then, the government officials have been termed as cannibals or demons. If the government-\nofficials turn against a person, then from the hard-earned capital of a lifetime, he has to lose his hand.\nOne has to.\nThe reality is that no one wants to pay income tax—even\ngovernment employees hesitate to pay it—but when adverse times arise, income tax is forcibly\nrecovered and the taxpayer is left wailing in vain. Sometimes, the one who is devoted to the desired object, the great father, the great grandfather, etc., experiences the characteristics of a dream. 17. Meaning of the word\n\nPerhaps—sometimes; Manorath-Upagata—achieved by imagination in the mind; Pitra—father; Pitamah-Adi—or Baba and others;\nAsat—even after being very dead (and not knowing that the soul has departed); Sat—father or grandfather returns again;\nIti—thinking thus; Swapna-nirvruti-lakshanam—like the pleasure of a dream; Anubhavati—(one) experiences. Sometimes a living being starts imagining that his father or Baba has re-appeared in this world as his son or\ngrandson. In this way, they experience the pleasure of a dream.\nAnd sometimes, a bound soul finds pleasure in such mental imaginations.\nMeaning: Because